WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.918
[Music starts] [Audio Description - not captioned]

00:00:04.066 --> 00:00:08.793
[Lisa Federer, PhD] Data Science basically
is just the set of methodologies and techniques

00:00:10.328 --> 00:00:13.595
that are used to extract knowledge from data.

00:00:17.543 --> 00:00:24.418
So, how do we get from a big pile of raw data
to something that's actually meaningful.

00:00:28.479 --> 00:00:32.599
[Maryam Zaringhalam, PhD] Part of making data
available to so many different audiences is

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:39.260
really building that scaffolding, that infrastructure,
for people to find data and then ultimately

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:40.334
make use of it.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:49.001
[Federer] NLM has been involved in data before
data science was even really a term.

00:00:49.001 --> 00:00:53.969
We have the National Center for Biotechnology
Information, which is a really incredible

00:00:53.969 --> 00:00:58.835
data resource for people that are working
with biomedical data of all different types.

00:00:58.835 --> 00:01:03.208
And we have a lot of other resources that
we make available, like PubMed, to provide

00:01:03.208 --> 00:01:04.701
access to the literature.

00:01:04.701 --> 00:01:11.330
So really, we have been involved in making
information and data available to people for

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:12.376
a long time.

00:01:16.384 --> 00:01:21.920
[Zaringhalam] We're in this moment where we
have genomics technologies and we have wearables

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.060
that are collecting information from us.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:28.950
We have computerized clinical records and
all of these different data sources 

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:33.960
that, if we could combine them and figure out you
know what are the best forms of analysis,

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:39.464
we can really accelerate the speed of biomedical
discovery.

00:01:39.464 --> 00:01:44.459
[Federer] And we have the kind of expertise
at NLM that allows us to, not only make data

00:01:44.459 --> 00:01:48.258
available, but do research that really advances
data science.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.670
Particularly in the areas of things like natural
language processing, which involves using

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:01.376
data science techniques to extract information
from text.

00:02:01.699 --> 00:02:06.369
[Zaringhalam] When we think of a library,
we sort of think of books on a shelf and librarians

00:02:06.369 --> 00:02:08.524
as people handing books 
to us.

00:02:16.696 --> 00:02:23.959
But really, NLM has this long track record
of making data available to different users

00:02:23.959 --> 00:02:26.408
in all of these different types of ways.

00:02:26.408 --> 00:02:30.208
[Federer] Like any library, we exist to make
information available to people. And 

00:02:30.208 --> 00:02:34.380
now, we're doing that in a way that fits
with the modern techniques and technologies

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:37.115
that we have available to us through data
science approaches.

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:42.539
[Music]

00:02:42.539 --> 00:02:43.395
[Music fades to silence]

